    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Hawaiian grew from the speech of Polynesian settlers, Marquesans arriving in roughly 300 CE by one account, and is closest to Marquesan and Tahitian.</li><li>Written down by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> missionaries from the 1820s, it became the language of a literate kingdom, of its constitution, newspapers and schools.</li><li>After the overthrow of the monarchy, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>’s Act 57 of 1896 made English the language of the schools and children were punished for speaking Hawaiian; immersion schools since 1984 have begun to bring it back.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Hawaiian-medium schools teach the shape of a sentence as a pepeke, a squid: its head (poʻo) stands for the predicate, the point where head and tentacles meet (piko) for the subject, and the tentacles (ʻawe) for the object.</p></aside>
    </section>

