      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 300</span><span class="rf-text">Marquesans colonise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaiian Islands</a>, by one account, followed by later waves from the Society Islands and Samoa-Tonga</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1778</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a>’s expedition makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first recorded contact with Hawaiʻi and writes the island’s name as Owhyhee</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1820</span><span class="rf-text">Protestant missionaries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> arrive and set about creating a written Hawaiian</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1834</span><span class="rf-text">The first Hawaiian-language newspapers are published</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1839</span><span class="rf-text">The Hawaiian Bible is completed, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-iii">Kamehameha III</a> establishes the first Hawaiian-language constitution, in 1839 and 1840</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1896</span><span class="rf-text">The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>’s Act 57 makes English the medium of instruction in all public and private schools</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1984</span><span class="rf-text">The ʻAha Pūnana Leo opens its first Hawaiian-language immersion preschool</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1987</span><span class="rf-text">Hawaiian is again allowed as a medium of instruction in public schools, after 91 years</span></li></ol>
